lessons.push({
  title: "Binary Search Trees: Insert, Search, Validate",
  minutes: 55,
  learn: ["State the binary search tree property", "Insert, search, find min/max and traverse a BST", "Validate whether a tree is a BST", "Explain why balance matters and name balanced trees"],
  why: "A BST keeps data sorted while allowing fast search, insert and delete. It is the idea behind ordered maps, databases and file indexes, and a staple of interviews.",
  realWorld: "An ordered dictionary of contacts, a scheduler that always needs the next event in time order, range queries (all items between a and b).",
  simple: "In a BST every value in a node's <strong>left</strong> subtree is smaller than the node, and every value in its <strong>right</strong> subtree is larger. So a search can go left or right at each step and discard half of the remaining tree, like binary search.",
  detail: `<h3>Operations</h3>
${table(["Operation", "Idea", "Balanced", "Worst (a chain)"], [["search", "compare, go left or right", "O(log n)", "O(n)"], ["insert", "search for the empty spot, attach a new leaf", "O(log n)", "O(n)"], ["min / max", "keep going left / right", "O(log n)", "O(n)"], ["inorder traversal", "visits values in sorted order", "O(n)", "O(n)"], ["delete", "leaf: remove; one child: replace by it; two children: replace by the inorder successor", "O(log n)", "O(n)"]])}
<p>All costs are O(h), the height. Inserting already-sorted data into a plain BST makes a chain (h = n). <strong>Self-balancing</strong> trees (AVL, red-black) rotate to keep h = O(log n); B-trees are the disk-friendly variant behind database indexes. Python has no built-in BST; use <code>sortedcontainers</code> (third-party) or <code>bisect</code> on a list when you need ordered data.</p>
<h3>Validating a BST</h3>
<p>Checking only a node against its direct children is wrong: 6 sitting in the right subtree of 10 must be rejected even though it is greater than its parent 5. Pass down a valid range <code>(low, high)</code>: every node must satisfy <code>low &lt; val &lt; high</code>; going left tightens <code>high</code>, going right tightens <code>low</code>. Equivalent test: the inorder traversal is strictly increasing.</p>
<h3>Duplicates</h3>
<p>Decide a rule (ignore them, count them, or send them to the right) and state it.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `def insert(node, val):\n    if node is None:\n        return TreeNode(val)\n    if val < node.val:\n        node.left = insert(node.left, val)\n    elif val > node.val:\n        node.right = insert(node.right, val)\n    return node`, note: "Returns the (possibly new) subtree root." },
  diagram: graph("A binary search tree", "Root 8; left subtree 3 with children 1 and 6; right subtree 10 with a right child 14; every left value is smaller and every right value larger.", 600, 250,
    [node("a", "8", 300, 36), node("b", "3", 170, 115), node("c", "10", 430, 115), node("d", "1", 100, 200), node("e", "6", 240, 200), node("f", "14", 500, 200)],
    [{ from: "a", to: "b" }, { from: "a", to: "c" }, { from: "b", to: "d" }, { from: "b", to: "e" }, { from: "c", to: "f" }]),
  example: { code: TREE_SETUP + `\ndef insert(node, val):\n    if node is None:\n        return TreeNode(val)\n    if val < node.val:\n        node.left = insert(node.left, val)\n    elif val > node.val:\n        node.right = insert(node.right, val)\n    return node\n\ndef search(node, val):\n    while node and node.val != val:\n        node = node.left if val < node.val else node.right\n    return node is not None\n\ndef inorder(n):\n    return [] if n is None else inorder(n.left) + [n.val] + inorder(n.right)\ndef height(n):\n    return 0 if n is None else 1 + max(height(n.left), height(n.right))\n\ndef is_bst(node, low=float("-inf"), high=float("inf")):\n    if node is None:\n        return True\n    return low < node.val < high and is_bst(node.left, low, node.val) and is_bst(node.right, node.val, high)\n\nroot = None\nfor v in [8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14, 3]:\n    root = insert(root, v)\nprint(inorder(root), height(root))\nprint(search(root, 6), search(root, 7))\nprint(is_bst(root), is_bst(build_tree("10 5 15 null null 6 20".split())), is_bst(build_tree("2 1 3".split())))\nchain = None\nfor v in range(1, 6):\n    chain = insert(chain, v)\nprint(height(chain))`, out: `[1, 3, 6, 8, 10, 14] 3\nTrue False\nTrue False True\n5` },
  lineByLine: [["insert", "Recurses to the empty position and returns the root of each subtree; a duplicate (3 again) is ignored."], ["search", "A loop that goes left or right until the value or an empty spot is reached."], ["inorder gives sorted values", "The BST property in action."], ["is_bst with (low, high)", "Each node must fall strictly between the bounds inherited from its ancestors."], ["chain of 1..5", "Sorted insertions produce a tree of height n: O(n) operations."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Inserting 6 into the BST that already holds 8, 3, 10, 1:", headers: ["node", "compare", "go"], rows: [["8", "6 < 8", "left"], ["3", "6 > 3", "right"], ["empty spot", "-", "attach 6 as the right child of 3"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Range query on a BST: all values between lo and hi, pruning whole subtrees.", code: `import sys\n` + TREE_SETUP.split("\n\ndef build_tree")[0].replace("from collections import deque\n\n", "") + `\ndef insert(node, val):\n    if node is None:\n        return TreeNode(val)\n    if val < node.val:\n        node.left = insert(node.left, val)\n    else:\n        node.right = insert(node.right, val)\n    return node\ndef in_range(node, lo, hi):\n    if node is None:\n        return []\n    out = in_range(node.left, lo, hi) if node.val > lo else []\n    if lo <= node.val <= hi:\n        out.append(node.val)\n    return out + (in_range(node.right, lo, hi) if node.val < hi else [])\nroot = None\nfor v in [8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14]:\n    root = insert(root, v)\nprint(in_range(root, 4, 10))`, out: `[6, 8, 10]` },
  mistakes: ["Validating only against the parent.", "Forgetting that sorted input degrades a plain BST to a list.", "Not deciding how to treat duplicates.", "Forgetting to return the node from a recursive insert.", "Assuming O(log n) without balancing."],
  practices: ["Use the (low, high) bound technique.", "Use inorder to check sortedness.", "In real code prefer dict, set, bisect or sortedcontainers."],
  interview: ["Validate a BST.", "Find the k-th smallest element in a BST.", "Lowest common ancestor in a BST.", "Why do we balance trees?", "Convert a sorted array to a balanced BST."],
  practiceQs: ["What is the inorder traversal of a BST?", "What is the height after inserting 1, 2, 3, 4 in order?", "How do you find the successor of a node?", "Search for 7 in the example tree: which nodes are visited?"],
  revision: ["Left < node < right.", "Search/insert/delete O(h); h from log n to n.", "Inorder = sorted.", "Validate with bounds; balance with AVL or red-black."],
  hinglish: "BST mein har node ke left ke sab value chhote aur right ke sab value bade hote hain, isliye search mein har baar aadha tree chhod dete hain. Inorder traversal sorted value deta hai. Sorted data ek ek karke daalne par tree ek lambi chain ban jata hai.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What does an inorder traversal of a BST produce?", ["Random order", "Values in sorted order", "Levels", "Reversed order"], 1, "Left, node, right respects the property."),
    mcq("What is the worst-case search time in an unbalanced BST?", ["O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(n log n)"], 2, "A chain has height n."),
    mcq("Why is checking only node versus parent insufficient to validate a BST?", ["It is slower", "A deeper node can violate an ancestor's bound", "BSTs have no parents", "It is fine"], 1, "The whole subtree must respect the ancestor."),
    mcq("Which trees keep height O(log n) automatically?", ["Plain BSTs", "AVL and red-black trees", "Linked lists", "Stacks"], 1, "They rotate to rebalance."),
    mcq("What does the height become after inserting 1, 2, 3, 4 into an empty plain BST?", ["2", "3", "4", "1"], 2, "A right-leaning chain.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION")
  ],
  tasks: [
    task("Build a BST and report", "Read a line of distinct integers. Insert them one by one into an initially empty binary search tree (smaller values left, larger right). Print the inorder traversal on the first line and the height (number of nodes on the longest path) on the second line.", "class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val, self.left, self.right = val, None, None\n\nvalues = list(map(int, input().split()))\n# insert into a BST, then print inorder and height\n", `class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val, self.left, self.right = val, None, None\n\ndef insert(node, val):\n    if node is None:\n        return TreeNode(val)\n    if val < node.val:\n        node.left = insert(node.left, val)\n    else:\n        node.right = insert(node.right, val)\n    return node\n\ndef inorder(n):\n    return [] if n is None else inorder(n.left) + [n.val] + inorder(n.right)\n\ndef height(n):\n    return 0 if n is None else 1 + max(height(n.left), height(n.right))\n\nroot = None\nfor v in map(int, input().split()):\n    root = insert(root, v)\nprint(" ".join(map(str, inorder(root))))\nprint(height(root))`, [["5 3 8 1 4 9", "1 3 4 5 8 9\n3"], ["1 2 3 4", "1 2 3 4\n4"]], [["5 3 8 1 4 9", "1 3 4 5 8 9\n3"], ["1 2 3 4", "1 2 3 4\n4"], ["7", "7\n1"], ["4 2 6 1 3 5 7", "1 2 3 4 5 6 7\n3"], ["10 5 15 3 7 12 20 1", "1 3 5 7 10 12 15 20\n4"]]),
    task("Is it a binary search tree?", "The input is a level-order list (null marks a missing child). Print True if the tree is a valid binary search tree (strictly smaller on the left, strictly larger on the right, for every ancestor), otherwise False.", TREE_SETUP + "\nroot = build_tree(input().split())\n# validate with bounds\n", TREE_SETUP + `\ndef valid(node, low=float("-inf"), high=float("inf")):\n    if node is None:\n        return True\n    return low < node.val < high and valid(node.left, low, node.val) and valid(node.right, node.val, high)\n\nprint(valid(build_tree(input().split())))`, [["2 1 3", "True"], ["5 1 4 null null 3 6", "False"]], [["2 1 3", "True"], ["5 1 4 null null 3 6", "False"], ["10 5 15 null null 6 20", "False"], ["1", "True"], ["2 2 2", "False"], ["8 3 10 1 6 null 14", "True"]]),
  ],
});

